There were [1mtwo categories of bugs[22m in the C-to-Rust translation:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m### Bug 1: Buffer position tracking in [38;5;10mblake256_update[0m and [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
In the C code, the [38;5;10mleft[0m variable (tracking how many bytes are already buffered) is explicitly set to [38;5;10m0[0m after the first if-block processes a partial buffer:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mc
[0m[38;5;10mdata += fill;
datalen -= (fill << 3);
left = 0;  // <-- critical reset
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust translation used an immutable [38;5;10mleft[0m and tried to compensate with a [38;5;10mnew_left[0m variable using a broken heuristic:[0m[0m
[1mrust
[0m[38;5;10mlet new_left = if left != 0 && ((data[..].len() - data_off) > 0) { 0 } else { left };
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This condition was wrong — it checked whether there was remaining data rather than whether the first if-block had fired. The fix was to make [38;5;10mleft[0m mutable and set it to [38;5;10m0[0m after the first if-block, exactly matching the C logic.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mFiles changed:[22m [38;5;10mblake256.rs[0m, [38;5;10mblake512.rs[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m### Bug 2: Bit-length vs byte-length confusion in [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m calls[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The C [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m (and [38;5;10mblake256_update[0m) takes [38;5;10mdatalen[0m in [1mbits[22m. The one-shot [38;5;10mblake512()[0m function correctly multiplies by 8 before calling [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m. However, the C code in [38;5;10mhash_blake.c[0m and [38;5;10mPQCgenKAT_sign.c[0m calls [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m directly with [1mbyte-lengths[22m (not multiplied by 8), which is the convention used throughout the SPHINCS+ codebase for direct update calls.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
